<li><strong>Days 1–15:</strong> choose only connected food/packaging machinery OEMs and screen twenty accounts for own-brand products, controlled software releases, field support and no dedicated product-security operation. Secure one product-security lawyer and one machinery/CE adviser who will review boundaries, not decorate the website.</li>
<li><strong>Days 16–30:</strong> conduct ten 45-minute workflow interviews with engineering, quality and product leaders. Reconstruct the last real release, vulnerability, customer security request and product-data request. Ask who did the work, hours lost, deadline, budget owner and which supplier was paid. Do not pitch until the reconstruction is complete.</li>
<li><strong>Days 31–45:</strong> sell the €7,500 baseline to all qualified interviewees. Offer the same scope and price. Maximum total pre-evidence spend is €5,000 for counsel review, insurance advice, travel and a disposable prototype; no platform build, certification or employee.</li>
<li><strong>Days 46–75:</strong> deliver at most two paid baselines in the customer's existing tools. Time every task and mark each decision as legal, manufacturer risk, technical implementation or routine operation. Run the incident clock and one data-access path with real product evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Days 76–90:</strong> ask each buyer to sign a six-month managed-operation pilot at €3,000 a month, billed monthly in advance. The pass rule is two paid baselines from ten qualified offers and at least one retainer signed, with 60% of forecast recurring work classed as analyst/engineer work rather than founder judgement. The kill rule is no paid baseline after ten qualified offers, or two baselines that expose less than one day a month of recurring external work.</li>
</ol>
