+<!DOCTYPE html>
+<html lang="en">
+<head>
+<meta charset="utf-8">
+<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+<title>A shared limit is a real operating system — The Second Engine</title>
+<meta name="description" content="A test of a control-and-evidence layer for Dutch business-park energy hubs, and of whether it can retain margin beside installers, traders and grid operators.">
+<link rel="stylesheet" href="../static/style.css">
+<script src="../static/theme.js"></script>
+</head>
+<body>
+<header class="site-header">
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <a class="brand" href="../index.html">The Second <span>Engine</span></a>
+    <nav class="site-nav">
+      <a href="../index.html">Contents</a>
+      <a href="../about.html">Method</a>
+      <button class="theme-toggle" type="button">☾ Dark</button>
+    </nav>
+  </div>
+</header>
+<main class="wrap">
+<p class="kicker">Essay 06 of 7 · Grid-congestion Capacity Control for Business Parks</p>
+<h1>A shared limit is a real operating system, not an energy dashboard</h1>
+<p class="lede">A factory that cannot electrify a process, add a charger depot or energise an expansion has a purchase event; a sustainability officer does not. Dutch energy hubs now have a live group-transport contract route, but its availability, technical prerequisites and timetable differ by grid operator. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-final --> <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> The opportunity is a control-and-evidence layer that helps a qualified group stay inside its shared capacity while explaining every intervention. The fatal possibility is that the installer, trader, aggregator or grid operator already owns the controls, the hardware and the Dutch-language relationship. This is therefore not a thesis about selling optimisation software to every business park. It is a test of whether one blocked site will pay for a neutral, contract-aware operating layer through the party it already trusts.</p>
+
+<section class="proposition">
+<h2>The proposition</h2>
+<p>The first customer is a group of four to eight large connections behind one eligible electricity station in Liander territory: an existing business park with one member whose signed expansion, electrified fleet, cold store or production equipment is blocked by transport capacity. The economic buyer is that member's operations or investment director, with park management as organiser. The group, not an individual sustainability lead, must choose who may use shared headroom and who pays when it is scarce.</p>
+<p>The product is a fixed-scope <em>capacity operating baseline</em>, followed by a managed control-and-evidence service. It ingests revenue-meter and local-controller readings; reconciles the contractual group limit, each member's agreed allocation and the current operating state; issues a proposed curtailment order; records acknowledgement, command, response and override; and produces a monthly exception record for the group. It does not trade energy, sell a battery, procure a grid connection, promise additional capacity, or sign the group's transport contract.</p>
+<p>The practical boundary is important. A group transport agreement shares transport capacity; it does not make unrelated sites one electrical installation. At Enexis, each participant retains its own connection, the group has one transport contract, and an energy-management system is required to apply the allocation and remain within the group capacity. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-gto --> The business can connect to an installer's battery, charger, building-management or process controller, but it must not take over safety interlocks or process sequencing. The equipment owner retains final local authority.</p>
+<p>The substitute is substantial: a park manager's spreadsheet and WhatsApp group; an installer-supplied energy-management system; a trader or aggregator's virtual power plant; a consultant's feasibility model; or a member choosing a smaller project and keeping its individual limit. The proposed layer wins only when it makes the shared promise more operable and auditable than those substitutes. A prettier load chart does not justify a new supplier.</p>
+</section>
+
+<section class="changed">
+<h2>What changed</h2>
+<p>The contract position is no longer merely a policy proposal. ACM published its final code decision on the group transport agreement on 19 December 2025, and says companies in an energy hub can apply to their grid operator for a GTO that lets them share transport capacity and coordinate local demand and supply. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-final --> That establishes a contract route; it does not establish that every park can obtain a useful limit or that any particular control vendor is approved.</p>
+<p>Implementation is uneven. Liander describes a current group-contract application route for large connections above 3×80 A, requiring a formed hub, high-frequency metering and all members on the same electricity station. It says at least one member must have contracted transport capacity and gives an average two-year lead time for forming the group and agreeing the contract. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> Enexis says it is working with ten business parks and expects to offer GTOs more broadly from 2027. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-rollout --> An entrant should therefore sell neither “a Dutch GTO product” nor a deadline. It should qualify the operator, station topology, connection class and application status before a software proposal.</p>
+<p>Other live forms are different tools, not synonyms. ACM said regional grid operators could offer time-block-bound transport contracts from 1 April 2025; the customer may use the connection only in agreed blocks. On the transmission network, a duration-bound form permits maximum transport at least 85% of the time. <!-- CHECK: 06-atr-date --> ACM's current dashboard also distinguishes capacity-restriction and capacity-steering contracts, group variants, time-block-bound and time-duration-bound rights, and a fully non-firm right dependent on actual grid loading. <!-- CHECK: 06-contract-taxonomy --> A project that needs a firm evening peak cannot quietly be sold a time-block product and called solved.</p>
+<p>Capacity-restriction contracts are live as well. Liander describes a CBC with call-up, where the customer agrees a maximum flexible quantity and is asked to adjust it, and a CBC without call-up, where the change happens at fixed agreed times; it also describes redispatch through GOPACS and a Congestion Service Provider. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-cbc --> These contracts can create a control obligation and a payment mechanism, but neither proves that a business park will fund a separate evidence layer.</p>
+<p>The public response is material but is not customer revenue. Rijksoverheid says €62 million is available for advice and investments for organisations blocked by a full grid, and €166 million for stimulating energy hubs. <!-- CHECK: 06-public-support --> Those are programme budgets, not a price list, award to this company, or proof that an installer will share margin. The demand evidence that matters is narrower: a member has a blocked, costed investment and the group has reached a contract and governance choice that must be operated.</p>
+</section>
+
+<section class="sale">
+<h2>The first sale</h2>
+<p>Make ten named-prospect types from an actual operator map: park-management foundations in Liander congestion areas; industrial estates with an approved hub study; cold-storage, food-processing, logistics, metals, waste-processing and fleet-depot anchor tenants; and the electrical installer or energy adviser already analysing their profiles. Screen out parks without common station eligibility, a committed anchor investment, or agreement to share interval data. An ambitious solar plan is not enough.</p>
+<p>The opening route is through a Dutch-speaking electrical installer and an energy consultant, not independent English-language outbound. They already explain switchgear, meter access, permits, operation limits and member politics. The founder can lead integration design and a transparent evidence model in English; the channel partner must lead Dutch workshops, contractual discovery and on-call discussions. If the partner asks the founder to become the park's Dutch-speaking energy manager, the model violates the reader's constraint and should be declined.</p>
+<p>Sell a four-week, <strong>€6,000 paid capacity operating baseline</strong>. That is a required test price, not an observed market rate. It reconciles fifteen-minute data against the group's proposed limit; maps controllable loads and their local owners; rehearses one safe curtailment path in a test window; specifies the command/acknowledgement/evidence log; and produces an exception and liability schedule. It excludes hardware, switchgear changes, protection settings, a grid-operator application, energy-market trading and any promise that the hub will be admitted.</p>
+<p>The signers should be the anchor member's investment director and the hub entity or delegated park manager, because a control order reallocates a shared commercial right. The production offer is a required hurdle of <strong>€20,000 onboarding plus €4,500 per live site per month</strong>, billed through the installer under a disclosed wholesale schedule. Hardware and installation remain on the installer's invoice. Before proposing it, require a written allocation policy, a named 24/7 escalation contact at every member, a controller test interface, and an agreed answer to one question: who bears the commercial consequence when a member overrides a curtailment instruction?</p>
+</section>
+
+<section class="economics">
+<h2>Does the money work?</h2>
+<p>This is design arithmetic, not a survey of Dutch prices. Every price, conversion and cost below is a validation hurdle. The model deliberately keeps batteries, meters, gateways, electrical works and installation off this company's revenue and warranty line.</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
+<thead><tr><th>€000</th><th>Year 1 design hurdle</th><th>Mature design hurdle</th></tr></thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td>Paid baselines</td><td>3 × €6k = 18</td><td>8 × €8k = 64</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Onboarding</td><td>1 × €20k = 20</td><td>5 × €20k = 100</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Recurring control/evidence</td><td>3 sites × 6 months × €4.5k = 81</td><td>15 sites × 12 months × €4.5k = 810</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Revenue</strong></td><td><strong>119</strong></td><td><strong>974</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Pass-through hardware and installation</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Direct delivery: engineers, specialist cover, cloud</td><td>55</td><td>245</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Gross margin</strong></td><td><strong>64 (54%)</strong></td><td><strong>729 (75%)</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td>Insurance, security, partner enablement, travel, admin</td><td>43</td><td>100</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Founder labour at replacement cost</td><td>85</td><td>110</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Operating surplus after founder replacement</strong></td><td><strong>−64</strong></td><td><strong>519</strong></td></tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Year one does not pay the owner target. That is acceptable only because it tests a repeatable unit before hiring. The mature case reaches the €100,000 pre-personal-tax owner-compensation gate and leaves retained cash, but only if two delivery engineers can run fifteen sites while an on-call electrical-control specialist handles exceptions. That is 7.5 sites per engineer. It is a capacity hypothesis to time-log: count integrations, tested commands, exceptions, overrides and post-incident reviews per site. If each peak needs bespoke founder judgement, the margin is fictional.</p>
+<p>The 75% gross-margin hurdle is not licence economics. It pays for integration variance, secure hosting, observability, control testing, audit records, specialist review and a response path. A 25% wholesale discount on the €810,000 recurring line removes €202,500 before costs change. That is why the installer cannot simply be “the channel” and take whatever margin it requests. The wholesale card has to cover responsibilities and show the anchor member an outcome worth buying.</p>
+<p>Working capital is as important as the income statement. The design requires a maximum <strong>€60,000</strong> cash reserve for the first-year operating loss and receivables gap, plus <strong>€5,000</strong> of owned test equipment and secure connectivity. The production customer funds site-specific hardware directly and pays onboarding 50% in advance; recurring service is invoiced monthly in advance. Do not hire the second engineer, purchase a production gateway fleet or guarantee a response time before signed recurring receipts cover it. These are capital-allocation rules, not reported supplier terms.</p>
+</section>
+
+<section class="scale">
+<h2>From service to company</h2>
+<p><strong>Customer one</strong> is a site study with a thin control path. The founder traces meter identifiers, discovers whether a charger, battery or process load actually accepts an external setpoint, and watches a supervised test. The useful output is not a dashboard. It is a signed map from contractual constraint to meter, controller, local owner, safety boundary, permitted action, acknowledgement and recovery procedure.</p>
+<p><strong>Customer five</strong> uses a versioned connector kit for the few controller classes actually encountered, a standard allocation-policy schema and a repeatable test protocol. A trained implementation engineer can onboard data, perform reconciliation and produce an exception report. The electrical-control specialist approves control boundaries; the founder handles only unresolved integration and commercial escalation. The installer sells the baseline from a fixed scope, not from a fresh technical essay.</p>
+<p><strong>Customer twenty</strong> is possible only through three to five enabled installer/adviser partners. Its recurring state is real: members join or leave; allocations change; equipment is maintained; a controller is offline; a new load changes the profile; a call-up or fixed time block changes the permitted action; an override must be investigated; and a monthly group settlement needs evidence. The accumulating asset is a tested library of controller adapters, policy templates, simulator fixtures, failure modes and evidence records. Raw interval data belongs to the member or group under the agreed contracts.</p>
+<p>The four tests are severe. Acquisition repeats only when partners originate qualified baselines without the founder's personal introductions. Delivery is teachable only when someone other than the founder can pass a simulated command-and-recovery test from the playbook. Value recurs only because allocations, equipment and constraints change. The asset accumulates only when the next compatible controller and agreement can be onboarded faster without exposing a prior site's data. If two of these fail, call it specialist integration work and price it accordingly; do not call it a software company.</p>
+<p>Liability is the non-negotiable design issue. The service may promise data freshness, reconciliation, command dispatch, logged acknowledgement and escalation. It must not warrant uninterrupted production, grid admission, market revenue, electrical safety or that a local controller obeys. Each member owns its load, local safety system and emergency stop. The hub agreement needs priority and override rules, a cap on damages, exclusions for upstream communication and equipment faults, incident notification, audit access and a manual fallback. Insurance is useful only after those responsibilities are real in the operating record.</p>
+</section>
+
+<section class="competition">
+<h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
+<p>There is no empty category to enter. Sympower markets a stack that connects, aggregates, optimises and trades energy assets, including hardware or API interfaces, for commercial and industrial companies. <!-- CHECK: 06-sympower --> Withthegrid markets Dutch local control strategies including static and dynamic limitations, grid-operator endpoints and peak shaving. <!-- CHECK: 06-withthegrid --> Those are vendor descriptions, not independent evidence of outcomes, but they show that control, integration and market access are already purchasable together.</p>
+<p>Scholt Energy markets flexibility services around renewable generation, storage and flexible industrial consumption; it says its virtual power plant connects assets to energy markets and its Dutch CSP material describes controlling flexible installations with grid operators. <!-- CHECK: 06-scholt --> Enervalis' current portal markets itself as “Creating more value with energy.” <!-- CHECK: 06-enervalis --> That modest public claim is enough to count it as a real energy-software incumbent, not enough to infer a product feature or customer result.</p>
+<p>The more formidable incumbent is local. Liander publishes a group-contract route and says an energy-management system is used to coordinate use continuously. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> Enexis develops GTOs and hub tools through pilots, and says a technical adviser can assist a group before it proceeds. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-gto --> The grid operator cannot be displaced from capacity allocation; a well-equipped installer can retain the site controller; a trader can include flexibility dispatch; and a park manager can choose a consultant plus an EMS. Each can make the proposed layer look like an unnecessary extra integration.</p>
+<p>The narrow response is neutrality between equipment and market counterparties, not superiority in dispatch. Keep an independent contract-to-control evidence record, support the awkward mixed fleet an installer inherited, and make the installer more credible with its end customer. The partner receives branded reports, first-line relationship ownership and a clear wholesale margin. The new company retains the reconciliation engine, test fixtures, secure audit trail and specialist control policy. If the partner demands the source, the customer data and unrestricted reuse while paying a thin fee, it is buying know-how to internalise.</p>
+</section>
+
+<div class="counter">
+<h2>The capable failure</h2>
+<p>The company executes well. It finds an eligible park, maps the limits correctly, conducts safe control tests, documents overrides and never breaches a contracted group limit. The anchor tenant gets its investment moving. The installer presents the result to members, owns the hardware warranty and becomes the person called at 02:00. At renewal, the group treats evidence as a project appendix and asks the installer to fold the monitoring into its maintenance agreement.</p>
+<p>The installer then has the protocol, the controller mappings and a working relationship with the energy adviser. The trader offers dispatch and reporting as part of its flexibility contract. The GTO has made the operating problem clearer, but not proprietary. The evidence product has also made every exception legible enough for a competent integrator to copy. Customer count rises, yet each site demands partner-specific integration and the wholesale price falls. No sales failure occurred; the layer created value but did not control a budget, a contractual choke point or a learning asset that the channel could not reclaim.</p>
+</div>
+
+<section class="monday">
+<h2>The first ninety days</h2>
+<ol>
+<li><strong>Days 1–15:</strong> choose Liander territory only. Interview ten installer, energy-adviser, park-manager and anchor-member prospects. For each, capture the blocked investment, network operator, station topology, connection category, contract form considered, controller estate and decision owner. Spend no more than €2,000 on travel and Dutch technical assistance.</li>
+<li><strong>Days 10–25:</strong> retain an independent Dutch electrical-control engineer for up to €3,500 to define the safety boundary, test witness requirements and the manual-fallback protocol. If no qualified partner will own local safety and switching authority, stop before building control software.</li>
+<li><strong>Days 20–35:</strong> pay a Dutch energy-contract lawyer up to €3,500 to review one GTO or hub cooperation agreement and draft the allocation, override, evidence-access and liability schedule. Do not pretend a technical log substitutes for a member agreement.</li>
+<li><strong>Days 30–55:</strong> build only a simulator, interval-data reconciler, policy store, command/acknowledgement log and monthly exception report. Cap build, secure hosting and test connectivity at €6,000. No production gateway fleet, optimiser, trading desk or battery purchase.</li>
+<li><strong>Days 45–70:</strong> offer the same <strong>€6,000 paid baseline</strong>, 50% in advance, to five qualified installer-led groups. It is valid only where an anchor member will expose redacted interval data and a safe controller-test interface.</li>
+<li><strong>Days 70–90:</strong> deliver one baseline and seek the stated €20,000 onboarding plus €4,500 monthly production hurdle through the installer. Total pre-evidence spend is capped at <strong>€15,000</strong>.</li>
+</ol>
+<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one qualified group pays the baseline, completes a witnessed safe-control rehearsal, and the anchor member and installer sign a production order or dated authority to proceed at no less than the stated onboarding and recurring hurdles, with written allocation and liability terms. <strong>Kill:</strong> no paid baseline after five qualified offers; no group can name a blocked investment and eligible connection arrangement; or installers will sell only if they own all evidence, customer contact and reusable control work. In any kill state, offer occasional integration consulting only if premium self-employment is acceptable—do not call it the second engine.</p>
+</section>
+
+<div class="verdict-box">
+<h2>Verdict</h2>
+<p class="verdict"><strong>TEST THROUGH A PARTNER</strong></p>
+<p>The single assumption that would most change the verdict is that an installer or energy adviser can place a recurring, separately priced control-and-evidence layer with a blocked anchor tenant rather than bundling it into hardware, trading or its own engineering hours.</p>
+</div>
+
+<div class="reading">
+<h2>Reading</h2>
+<ul>
+<li>ACM, <a href="https://www.acm.nl/nl/publicaties/codebesluit-groepstransportovereenkomst">Codebesluit groepstransportovereenkomst</a> and <a href="https://www.acm.nl/nl/publicaties/acm-energiehubs-kunnen-groepstransportovereenkomst-aanvragen-bij-hun-netbeheerder">announcement of GTO applications</a>.</li>
+<li>Liander, <a href="https://www.liander.nl/grootzakelijk/energietransitie/samenwerken/transportovereenkomst-voor-groepen">Groepscontracten: zo werkt het</a> and <a href="https://www.liander.nl/grootzakelijk/energietransitie/alles-over-netcongestie/keuzehulp-deelnemen-congestiemanagement">congestion-management choice guide</a>.</li>
+<li>ACM, <a href="https://www.acm.nl/nl/publicaties/acm-stelt-maatregelen-tegen-netcongestie-vast-en-publiceert-uitgangspunten-voor-toekomstige-tariefaanpassingen">alternative transport rights decision</a> and <a href="https://www.acm.nl/nl/publicaties/voortgangsdashboard-bestuurlijke-afspraken-en-verbeterplannen-netbeheerders">implementation dashboard</a>.</li>
+<li>Enexis, <a href="https://www.enexis.nl/zakelijk/netcapaciteit/flexibiliteitsoplossingen/groepstransportovereenkomst">Groepstransportovereenkomst</a> and <a href="https://www.enexis.nl/over-ons/wat-doen-wij/onze-innovaties/energiehubs">energy-hub pilots</a>.</li>
+<li>Rijksoverheid, <a href="https://www.rijksoverheid.nl/themas/klimaat-milieu-en-natuur/duurzame-energie/kabinet-neemt-maatregelen-tegen-vol-elektriciteitsnet-netcongestie">measures for a full electricity grid</a>.</li>
+<li>Sympower, <a href="https://sympower.net/">platform overview</a>; Withthegrid, <a href="https://withthegrid.com/teleport/whitepaper/">Dutch congestion-control guide</a>; Scholt Energy, <a href="https://scholt.com/flex-services-for-energy-assets-scholt-energy">Flex Services</a>; Enervalis, <a href="https://portal.enervalis.com/">portal</a>. These are vendor descriptions, not independent performance evidence.</li>
+</ul>
+</div>
+
+<nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Essay navigation">
